{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target inverts the actual result. It asserts that 'most mutations were caught by tests that accepted the mutated subject as equivalent to the original, so the tests described rather than constrained,' attributing constraint to acceptance-with-rejection. But the problem context and evidence show the opposite: compile.py lost 1/9 mutations *because* installed guards were shown to fail first (the acceptance record was complete there); the ordinarily-guarded modules (4/4, 6/7, etc.) caught mutations precisely when the test *rejected* the mutant outright rather than accepting it. Constraint arises from the rejection itself being recorded, not from 'acceptance after rejection' as the target claims. By framing compile.py's guard failure as 'the clearest case' of constraint, the target misidentifies the mechanism that actually discriminates constrained from described outcomes in the 3-of-26 distribution.","target":"756f0844e91ccbedaa242be83da5fbe51648c2dd1540709beb149f0576bbd901"}]}